{"t":1793019549,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"36379118-59a9-4d69-ae76-73bffc51a908","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-26T06:14:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 260614\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n214 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nPatchy shallow fog has been developing early this morning, and \nmay expand a bit before lifting quickly after daybreak.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry through the end of October.\n \n- 2) Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through the end of October.\n\nLarge scale ridging persists at the surface and aloft through\nthe end of the week. This results in dry weather and above\nnormal temperatures through the end of October.\n\nSurface high pressure of Canadian origin that built over the \nMid-Atlantic this past weekend will drift south and west across \nthe central Appalachians and Tennessee Valley through tonight. \nThis continues a gradual warming trend that began on Sunday.\n\nA weakening cold front will approach from the north tonight, \ntied to low pressure over the Canadian Maritimes. But without \nmuch forcing or moisture, no precipitation is expected. The \nfront will cross the region Tuesday morning, then stall and \nweaken, or perhaps lift northward as a weak warm front, through\nmid week. Warmer than normal temperatures will continue during \nthis time as a result.\n\nAnother large area of high pressure will build to the north for\nthe second half of the week. The position of the high should\nallow for southeast to southwest winds and continued warm\ntemperatures by late October standards. The warmest day\ncurrently appears to be Friday when highs in the mid to upper\n70s are possible.\n\nSaturday may be a few degrees cooler as clouds begin to increase\nfrom the west ahead of a trough developing over the middle of\nthe country. Highs will likely still be above normal into the\nlower 70s, however.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nTroughing developing over the western CONUS late this week looks\nto eject one or more pieces of shortwave energy this weekend. \nThis results in lowering heights and an approaching surface \nfrontal system as the calendar flips to November. There is a lot\nof timing spread in the guidance on when these waves approach \nour area, but some low precipitation chances do return to the \nforecast by the end of the upcoming weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIt's a bit of a tricky forecast this morning due to a number of\ncompeting factors surrounding potential fog. Low-level flow is\nlight out of the west, which typically inhibits fog, and the\nbackground airmass isn't terribly moist. However, very sharp\nsurface temperature inversions are evident early this morning,\nresulting in shallow saturation. Whether this results in shallow\nfog or just dew is the big question, with the greatest potential\nfor fog and IFR occurring right around daybreak when surface\ninversions are at their sharpest. Confidence is modest, but\ngiven IFR fog already developing in some sheltered piedmont\nlocations and widespread T/Td spreads of less than 2 degrees as\nof 06Z, have inserted 1-3 hour TEMPOs around daybreak for IFR.\nThe notion of at least brief IFR fog is supported by the RAP,\nHRRR, GLAMP, RRFS, and NAM/NAMNest...but not so much by the\nMAV/MET or WRF-ARW/HRW-FV3.\n\nOtherwise, VFR conditions are expected to prevail through the\nupcoming week. Some light onshore flow Tuesday night through\nThursday could result in some late night/early morning sub-VFR\npotential. Dry weather is anticipated with winds shifting from W\ntoday, to NE Tuesday, then SE to SW Wednesday through Friday.\n\nThe next chance of precipitation does not arrive until the\nsecond half of the upcoming weekend, but uncertainty is high at\nthis time.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nLight west to northwest winds are expected through this evening,\nwith a shift to the north and northeast and an increase behind a\ndry cold front late tonight into Tuesday morning. Brief SCA\nconditions are possible Tuesday morning, but whether it's long \nenough in duration to justify a Small Craft Advisory (versus a \nMarine Weather Statement) is in question.\n\nSoutheast to southwest winds are expected mid to late week as\nhigh pressure builds to the north and northeast. Some channeling\nis possible at times, though overall gradients look weak.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTide levels remain a bit elevated, but anomalies continue to\nslowly decrease overall. Further minor flooding is not expected\nthrough the first half of this week.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Frost Advisory until 9 AM EDT this morning for MDZ005-006-008-\n     503>508.\nVA...Frost Advisory until 9 AM EDT this morning for VAZ037>040-050-\n     051-053-055-056-501-502-506-526-527.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...DHOF\nAVIATION...DHOF\nMARINE...DHOF\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793019518,"days":{"2026-10-26":{"hi":70,"lo":49,"pop":0,"short":"Patchy Fog then Sunny"},"2026-10-27":{"hi":71,"lo":47,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":72,"lo":50,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":77,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":72,"lo":53,"pop":8,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":70,"lo":55,"pop":43,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793016000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[6.0,7.7,9.5,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0